        "Leyla: How different the hospital feels. In the daytime there are visitors, doctors doing their rounds, phones ringing "
        "constantly. At three in the morning it's quiet, but it isn't calm. You have fewer staff and no one to ask quickly, so "
        "every decision feels heavier.\n\n"
        "Interviewer: How does the night shift affect your health?\n\n"
        "Leyla: Honestly, it's the hardest part. Your body never fully gets used to sleeping during the day. I wear an eye mask, "
        "I have thick curtains, and I switch my phone to silent, but if a neighbour starts drilling at noon, my 'night' is "
        "over. I've learned to protect my sleep the way other people protect their savings.\n\n"
        "Interviewer: Then why have you stayed for so long?\n\n"
        "Leyla: Because of the patients. At night people are often frightened. They can't sleep, their families have gone "
        "home, and they want someone to talk to. During the day there's no time for that. At night I can sit with an elderly "
